<ArticleLayout slug="ai-content-beauty-skincare" ctaLabel="Send a brief" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">Use AI to build a campaign around the product. Keep the product’s appearance and any result shown on skin tied to evidence, even when the image contains no written claim.</p>

  <h2>Where does AI content for beauty brands fit?</h2>

  <p>AI content for beauty brands can extend settings, casting, and campaign variations, provided the finished image represents the product truthfully. hubStudio combines generated work with product photography and human review. Keep packaging, shades, and texture tied to approved references; use suitable evidence for efficacy claims. A generated result is not proof.</p>

  <p>Decide what each asset asks a buyer to believe. A bathroom scene may only set a mood, while a cream disappearing into skin suggests absorption. Fewer visible lines on a face can imply a result without any before-and-after caption. For a serum launch, commission the setting separately from any demonstration of what happens after application. A synthetic model can appear in the campaign; that image does not document someone using the serum.</p>

  <p>Brief a generated scene as an illustration. If an image is meant to show an observed result, supply the observation records and the supporting evidence before production.</p>

