  <p>The sign-off roles are recommendations, not statutory job titles. An asset doesn’t always need a shoot, but it does need to represent the product accurately. Review its characteristics as well as any implied effect. This article describes production practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <h2>How do cosmetics claims rules affect production?</h2>

  <p>Rules reviewed October 24, 2026. Check the market rules before commissioning a product demonstration. This table identifies the rules used here; it is not an exhaustive market-clearance checklist.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Market</th>
          <th scope="col">Primary rule or guidance</th>
          <th scope="col">What to check in the image</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">US</th>
          <td>FDA intended-use definitions; FTC advertising substantiation</td>
          <td>Does the picture imply treatment or a body-function change? Does evidence support the overall message?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">EU</th>
          <td>Cosmetics Regulation Article 20; common claims criteria</td>
          <td>Do pictures imply characteristics or functions the product lacks? Does the evidence match the depicted effect?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">UK</th>
          <td>ASA/CAP beauty-filter guidance</td>
          <td>Has a filter or retouch exaggerated the advertised product’s effect?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">China</th>
          <td>NMPA efficacy evaluation standard; local advertising guidance where applicable</td>
          <td>Does the visual stay within the product’s supported claim and relevant registration or filing information?</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>US: decide whether the claim changes the product’s status</h3>

  <p>The FDA distinguishes cosmetics from drugs by intended use. Cleansing or beautifying is different from treating disease or changing the body’s structure or function. Advertising can help establish that intended use. If an animation suggests that a cream rebuilds skin, get a classification review before approving the visual.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">FDA’s definitions distinguish appearance-related cosmetic uses from drug uses involving disease or body structure and function. The method is a reading of its intended-use guidance, not a test of an individual product.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: FDA, September 11, 2024. https://www.fda.gov/cosmetics/cosmetics-laws-regulations/it-cosmetic-drug-or-both-or-it-soap</p>

  <p>The FTC separately requires a reasonable basis for objective advertising claims before the ad runs, including claims conveyed by implication. If the ad communicates that testing proves a result, the support must match that message. The team should review image, headline, and caption together.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Objective advertising claims need prior substantiation, including implied claims. The method is a reading of the FTC’s policy on the reasonable-basis requirement and advertised levels of support.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Federal Trade Commission, November 23, 1984. https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/ftc-policy-statement-regarding-advertising-substantiation</p>

  <h3>EU: pictures count as claims</h3>

  <p>Article 20 of the Cosmetics Regulation covers pictures as well as words. They cannot imply characteristics or functions the product does not have. The common criteria also connect claims to evidence and prevent a depiction of performance from going beyond that support.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Regulation 655/2013 sets six common criteria: legal compliance, truthfulness, evidential support, honesty, fairness, and informed decision-making. The method is a reading of its annex alongside Article 20 of Regulation 1223/2009, which expressly includes pictures.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: European Union, July 10, 2013, and November 30, 2009. https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A32013R0655 https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A32009R1223</p>

  <p>An ingredient’s documented properties do not automatically establish the same effect in the finished formula. An ingredient moving through an animated skin cross-section may imply more than the approved copy says. Let the claims reviewer see the rough sequence before the team polishes it.</p>

  <h3>UK: a filter can change the claim</h3>

  <p>The ASA’s beauty-filter rulings of February 3, 2021 addressed tanning ads in which filters exaggerated the apparent product effect. Its subsequent guidance does not prohibit every filter. It asks whether the alteration misleads about what the advertised product can achieve.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A beauty filter becomes a problem when it misleadingly exaggerates product efficacy. The method is a reading of CAP’s explanation of the ASA’s February 2021 rulings, not a blanket prohibition on retouching.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: ASA/CAP, February 11, 2021. https://www.asa.org.uk/news/the-mis-use-of-social-media-beauty-filters-when-advertising-cosmetic-products.html</p>

  <p>Keep the source footage and record changes to skin tone or texture. Removing a distracting object is a different decision from smoothing the very lines a moisturizer is advertised to improve.</p>

  <h3>China: connect the asset to the claim’s evidence</h3>

  <p>The NMPA efficacy evaluation standard calls for scientific support for cosmetic efficacy claims. Evaluation methods depend on the claim. Certain sensory and physical-effect claims qualify for an exemption from publishing the evidence summary. Keep the conditions attached, including the labeling condition for purely physical effects. This exemption is not general permission to make unsupported claims.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The NMPA standard took effect May 1, 2021, with different summary-upload deadlines for products registered or filed at different times. The method is a reading of the complete notice and standard republished by the Yunnan drug regulator, including the summary exemptions.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: NMPA notice published April 9, 2021; official republication December 22, 2021. https://mpa.yn.gov.cn/zfxxgk/zcwj/qtwj/gjwj/202112/t20211222_1224866.html</p>

  <p>Beijing’s cosmetics advertising guidance makes the connection explicit: efficacy and safety advertising should agree with the relevant registration or filing information and evidence summary. That is local guidance, not a new nationwide platform rule.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Beijing’s guidance links efficacy and safety advertising to the product record and supporting summary. The method is a reading of the guidance’s content-accuracy provisions and its stated Beijing scope.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Beijing market and drug regulators, June 27, 2025. https://scjgj.beijing.gov.cn/zwxx/2024zcwj/202506/t20250627_4129056.html</p>

  <p><a href="/resources/insights/beauty-content-production-china">Beauty content production in China</a> covers the local production context in more depth. Approval in one market is not approval in another. Review the claim again before reusing it, even when the only planned change is a translated caption.</p>

  <h2>Do synthetic models need a separate disclosure review?</h2>

  <p>Yes. This review concerns invented performers; it does not clear the rights to a real person’s likeness. Review whether the performer must be identified as synthetic separately from whether the product message is true. The requirements depend on where the asset appears and what kind of performer it contains.</p>

  <p>Amazon’s official announcement for listings and A+ (enhanced product-page content) requires applicable media containing photorealistic AI-generated people to carry the <code>contains-synthetic-performer</code> keyword in the XMP <code>dc:subject</code> field before upload. The announcement excludes, among other categories, real people who were merely altered using AI. It is not a rule to label every edited bottle.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Amazon’s announcement specifies a metadata tag for photorealistic AI-generated people in listings and A+ content across its worldwide stores. The method is a reading of the official announcement and tagging guide.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Amazon Seller Central, July 27, 2026; guide checked October 24, 2026. https://sellercentral-europe.amazon.com/seller-forums/discussions/t/ed53566e-9add-4008-a7bd-2bca6f600fee?mons_sel_locale=en_GB https://sellercentral.amazon.com/help/hub/reference/external/GFXHCHYZRGJRBZA5?locale=en-US</p>

  <p>New York’s rule is already in effect. It covers a person in the business of selling property or services who creates an ad for a commercial purpose. When that person knows a synthetic performer is in the ad, the ad must conspicuously disclose it. Its definition concerns a human-like visual or audiovisual performer who is not recognizable as an identifiable natural person; audio-only ads and specified other uses have exclusions.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">New York’s synthetic-performer disclosure rule took effect June 9, 2026. The method is a reading of General Business Law 396-b and the governor’s dated commencement announcement.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: New York State, June 9, 2026; statute checked October 24, 2026. https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/396-B https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/governor-hochul-announces-first-nation-law-requiring-disclosure-when-advertisements-include-ai</p>

  <p>California’s forthcoming rule covers prominently featured synthetic performers and requires clear disclosure. The definition concerns a realistic human performance not recognizable as an identifiable real person, and includes voice. Review an audio ad under California’s definition; New York’s exclusion does not settle that question.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">California’s SB 1050 adds Business and Professions Code 17610. Its January 1, 2027 effective date follows from the September 16, 2026 enactment and the constitutional default for this regular-session statute. The method is a reading of the chaptered law and that effective-date rule.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: California Legislature, September 16, 2026; Article IV, section 8, in the legislature’s August 2022 handbook. https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB1050 https://www.senate.ca.gov/sites/senate.ca.gov/files/joint_handbook_2021-22_final.pdf</p>

  <p>Platform metadata and a conspicuous disclosure within an ad are separate delivery requirements. Check the applicable exclusions before selecting the final wording. <a href="/resources/insights/eu-ai-act-labeling-brand-content">EU AI Act labeling for brand content</a> addresses the separate European framework. Disclosing how an image was made leaves the product claim to be checked against its evidence.</p>

  <h2>Which assets belong in each channel’s brief?</h2>

  <p>Decide where the image will appear before requesting variations. A model portrait used as campaign imagery in a brand film may suggest a product result when it sits beside an efficacy claim on a shopping page. Ask the reviewer to approve the image in that placement, with the surrounding claim visible.</p>

