  <p>Use the <a href="/services/design/ecommerce">ecommerce design service page</a> to scope shopping assets, then the <a href="/solutions/platforms/amazon">Amazon platform page</a> if that marketplace is part of the launch. <a href="/resources/insights">Insights</a> has the broader production guides; <a href="/solutions/ai-production/image">AI image production</a> covers the service.</p>

  <h2>What should the reviewer receive before approving a batch?</h2>

  <p>The reviewer needs to compare the finished visual with the product and the evidence. Start with current pack photography and identify the formula or variant. Add the approved wording for the intended market, with the study or evidence reference that supports it. A general assurance that someone checked the claim leaves the reviewer unable to make that comparison. If support is missing, remove the proposed claim or revise the asset to stay within what the evidence supports.</p>

  <p>For a batch, match each claim variation to its evidence reference; don’t assume one approval covers every version. Put that reference beside the asset so the reviewer doesn’t have to search through the whole packet.</p>

  <p>A swatch needs its real reference and capture conditions. An on-skin result needs an account of what was observed, with any image processing explained. Even a real photo can mislead. Different lighting or makeup may change the impression of performance, as can the choice of subjects. The image alone doesn’t establish a product’s typical effect.</p>

  <p>Give the claims reviewer the final crop with its caption and on-image wording. Removing context can change what the viewer believes, so record the approved version and return to review when an edit changes the product effect or claim.</p>

  <p>Check the exported file too.</p>

  <p>If the destination requires metadata, check for it after resizing or converting the file. Keep the source reference and review record with that copy.</p>

  <h2>What do the beauty case studies demonstrate?</h2>

  <p>These are accounts of production work. They show how a campaign was made, not whether a cosmetic formula works.</p>

  <p>In the <a href="/work/noyz-mylk-de-parfum">Noyz case study</a>, a model trained on fluid physics depicts the mist-to-milk transformation. The master film was adapted for channel use. The simulated transformation is part of the campaign’s product story.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/work/age20">age20 case study</a> describes product photography becoming on-model campaigns across Asian markets, with regional adaptations. The published timing is specific to that project.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The age20 case study reports product photography becoming on-model campaign imagery in hours. This is hubStudio’s published account; it does not supply an independent timing study or a delivery guarantee.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio case study, checked October 24, 2026. https://www.hubstudio.ai/work/age20</p>

  <p>For <a href="/work/shiseido-rq-pyology">Shiseido RQ PYOLOGY</a>, the case study describes campaign visuals and brand video balancing clinical credibility with luxury appeal in China’s medical aesthetics market. That creative positioning does not establish regulatory approval for a product or claim.</p>

  <h2>How should a beauty team organize the work in hubStudio?</h2>

  <p>Choose the route around who will make the assets and who will approve them. Use the app when your own team can generate and edit the assets and own the review. Keep the approved product references close to the work.</p>

  <p><a href="/studio/with-the-app">Studio + app</a> suits a brand that wants the studio to produce the work while its reviewer approves in <a href="/help/validation">Validation</a>. The documented workflow lets a teammate or client approve an asset or send it back with a comment; versions and comments stay on the same thread. In the review comment, distinguish approval of the visual from approval of the claims. The app records the reviewer’s decision; it doesn’t supply legal clearance.</p>

  <p>Studio only is the route for commissioning finished work rather than doing the production yourself. Service delivery and approval still take place in hubStudio, through Client space or the customer’s workspace. In each route, name the person responsible for product accuracy and the person authorized to approve claims before production starts.</p>

  <h2>What else should a beauty team check before commissioning?</h2>

  <h3>Can beauty brands use AI-generated images in ads?</h3>

  <p>Yes, subject to the rules for the market, placement, product, and message. Generation can provide a campaign setting or an illustrative scene, but the finished ad must not mislead about the product. Review skin appearance, packaging, and implied results alongside the copy, then check any applicable synthetic-performer disclosure requirement.</p>

  <h3>Can I show AI-generated before and after skincare results?</h3>

  <p>Do not present an invented transformation as a person’s real result or as proof of efficacy. A synthetic comparison does not establish what the formula achieves. If the campaign needs a result demonstration, build it around appropriate evidence and comparable real records, and have the market reviewer assess the complete presentation before release.</p>

  <h3>Do AI models in beauty ads need to be disclosed?</h3>

  <p>Some uses do. New York has a synthetic-performer advertising rule, California has enacted a forthcoming rule, and Amazon requires metadata for applicable listing and A+ media. Their scopes differ. Check where the ad reaches consumers, whether the performer meets the definition, and which disclosure must survive the final export and upload.</p>

  <h3>Is AI skin retouching allowed in cosmetics advertising?</h3>

  <p>There is no universal permission for skin retouching. The UK guidance asks whether an alteration exaggerates the advertised product’s effect; other markets also assess truthful claims and supporting evidence. Keep the unaltered source so the reviewer can see what changed. Smoothing or recoloring must not distort the result the product is supposed to deliver.</p>

  <h3>What beauty content can AI make well?</h3>

  <p>Useful assignments include campaign environments, reviewed ingredient illustrations, model-led concepts, and adaptations built around approved product photography. Treat these as creative tasks with review, not a promise of perfect product fidelity. A shade, label, applicator, or texture that affects purchase expectations needs checking against the real product before the asset leaves the team.</p>

  <h3>How do cosmetics claims rules differ in the EU and the US?</h3>

  <p>In the US, intended use helps determine whether a product is a cosmetic, a drug, or both, while FTC rules govern advertising substantiation. EU cosmetics rules expressly address pictures and apply common claims criteria. Neither system treats a plausible generated image as evidence of efficacy. Check the product’s status and the exact visual message for each market.</p>

  <h2>What belongs in your production brief?</h2>

  <p>List the product variants and required placements so the production scope is clear. Send the product references, intended markets, and approved claims, together with the evidence owner and final reviewer. Identify which images illustrate the campaign and which are meant to show an observed product result.</p>
